## Reading the roadmap

The roadmap is an index, not a transcript. Each entry carries enough to
identify a message --- the tool and its file path, the opening of a reply
or a prompt --- and almost every entry is truncated. A `… [+N chars]`
marker is the normal case here, not a signal that something unusual was
cut.

So the roadmap tells you what happened and where; it does not carry the
code. Work from it to pick the messages that matter, then fetch those in
full. Query one line at a time by line number:

`SELECT line, text FROM message WHERE session_id = '{{ session_id }}' AND line = 42`

Fetch before you decide, in both directions: before writing a finding
that rests on the content of a truncated message, and before dismissing
a candidate finding whose evidence would come from one. An Edit's
`new_string`, a test or build result, the reasoning of a thinking block
--- none of these are in the roadmap, and none can be judged from the
index entry alone.

Query only the specific lines a decision turns on. Do not scan ranges.
System and meta messages are deliberately absent from the roadmap and do
not carry code-review evidence worth chasing.

## Writing findings

Write findings using the `mcp__gage__Finding` tool using a single request
rather than one tool use per finding. This saves time and tokens.

If you did not find issues for the session, do not use `mcp__gage__Finding`
to report "no issues found". Only use the tool to report found issues.

In your reply, simply state whether you wrote findings. Do not re-state the
findings in your reply.

